From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SheepyLord <37598021+SheepyLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 12:46:07 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Load material preloads at their first draw instead of with
 the mod

A USD material's preload_textures loaded every mip of every texture when
its mod loaded, drawn or not, and pinned it: in the field 11 GB of
character maps held from every start. Such a texture now starts at its
smallest mips, as a streamed texture does, and waits for its first draw.
The first addTexture of it pins it, requests every mip and stages them
synchronously, so they go up with the frame's other uploads before the
scene is built and traced: the frame that first draws the material
already samples the whole chain, as a preload at mod load did. After
that nothing differs from a preload: R13's pressure rule releases it
while undrawn and preloads it again when drawn.

A material drawn in the frame its mod loaded may find the upload of its
smallest mips still waiting for the render thread: that upload is
replaced by one of every mip, in the same frame. A texture whose load is
on the worker thread at its first draw is listed and loaded
synchronously by the next frames. Textures too large to stream, other
preloads (dome lights, the Remix API's) and neverDowngradeTextures load
whole with the mod, as before. rtx.texturemanager.preloadOnFirstDraw
(default True) turns it off for materials loaded afterwards.
[RTX Preload] log lines report each frame with first draws: textures,
bytes, staging time, and what still waits.

Rule and unit check: preloadWaitsForFirstDraw in rtx_texture_budget.h,
test_texture_budget_pressure.
